  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAny remaining isolated showers south of I-70 will exit the\nregion this evening as a cold front pushes south of the area.\nPatchy fog is possible, especially in areas where rain fell this\nafternoon, though dry advection with light nrly flow should\nlimit this potential for much of the area outside of the river\nvalleys. More seasonable lows and lower humidity levels are\nexpected tonight behind the front.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe remainder of the holiday weekend will provide a needed break\nfrom the convective parade our region has seen lately. Surface\nhigh pressure settling in from southeast Canada, with\nnortheasterly surface low. A good deal of sunshine is forecast\nboth Sunday and Labor Day, with temperatures remaining at\nseasonable levels.\n\n500mb heights rise after Monday as the northeast trough opens\nand departs, and as central CONUS ridging begins to build across\nthe Ohio Valley. This will lead to a warming trend for Tuesday\nand Wednesday, with continued dry weather. The next appreciable\nchance for rain may arrive with a cold front during the\nWednesday night/Thursday period.",
